.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
expr
- evaluate arguments as an algebraic expression
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
expr
arg ...
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
The arguments are taken as an expression.  After evaluation, the
result is written on the standard output.  Terms of the expression
must be separated by blanks.  Characters special to the shell - `*',
`&', `(' and `)' - must be escaped.
.sp
The operators and keywords, in order of increasing precedence, are:
.sp
.in +5
expr \|| expr
.br
expr \|& expr
.br
expr \|{ +, \*- } expr
.br
expr \|{ \\*, /, % } expr
.br
substr expr expr expr
.br
length expr
.br
index expr expr
.br
( expr )
.in -5
.sp
.it Substr
returns that portion of the first expression (possibly null) defined
by the offset (second expression, starting at 1) and the span (third
expression).  A large span gives the remainder of the string.
.sp
.it Length
returns the length in characters of the expression that follows.
.sp
.it Index
returns the character position of the first character of the first
expression that matches any character of the second, or `0' if none
matches.
.sp
Note that `0' is returned to indicate a zero value, rather than the
null string.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DIAGNOSTICS
.in +5
.br
`Non-numeric argument' if an argument needs to be, but is not, an
integer.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
